WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.310
After years in development and controversies
galore, "The Flash" is finally sprinting into

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:09.059
theaters, with way more characters – and
way more Batman – than even the rumor mill expected.

00:00:09.059 --> 00:00:13.091
Here are the details in the big game trailer
and TV spot that may have run right past you.

00:00:13.388 --> 00:00:17.340
"The Flash" trailer features appearances from
Batfleck and Michael Keaton as their respective

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.002
iterations of "The Dark Knight," which will
undoubtedly please fans of the SnyderVerse

00:00:21.002 --> 00:00:22.519
and Tim Burton's "Batman."

00:00:22.519 --> 00:00:26.039
That said, while it's exciting to see these
fine actors back in action, their trailer

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:28.640
cameos only confirm returns we've known about
for months.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.230
But are they the only Batmen in the trailer?

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:35.400
As of this video, it's unknown if Robert Pattinson
and Christian Bale will return as Batman in

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:35.900
"The Flash."

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.820
The rumors have been rampant for months, but
the trailer may have lent some meat to speculation.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:44.080
11 seconds into the teaser, there's a shot
of Gotham's resident hero soaring down the

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.080
street on his Batcycle.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:48.870
This part is interesting, as the street in
question is located in Glasgow, Scotland.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:53.070
Both "The Batman" and "The Dark Knight Rises"
used the Scottish city for some of their respective

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:54.500
Gotham City sequences.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:59.051
To be more specific, George Square is the
Glasgow location featured in the trailer.

00:00:59.051 --> 00:01:01.079
This street just so happened to be used in
"The Batman."

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:05.820
Sure, using the same location might be coincidental,
but let's not rule out the possibility of

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.123
Pattinson or Bale making an appearance.

00:01:08.337 --> 00:01:12.680
Batman has had his share of iconic live-action
theme songs over the years, but the top of

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:17.350
the heap could very well be the theme music
composed by Danny Elfman for the 1989 "Batman"

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:18.530
film directed by Tim Burton.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:22.360
That version of the Caped Crusader, played
by Michael Keaton, appears to offer help to

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.630
The Flash in his battle across alternate timelines.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:29.140
And just in case Keaton's distinctive growl
and cowl weren't enough, the music that accompanies

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.979
his on-screen entrance in the trailer is none
other than a rendition of that distinctive

00:01:32.979 --> 00:01:34.220
Elfman score.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.479
Keaton is featured quite prominently in the
trailer for "The Flash," and it's exciting

00:01:37.479 --> 00:01:41.591
enough for fans of his turns as Bruce Wayne
in "Batman" and "Batman Returns" to see him

00:01:41.591 --> 00:01:42.790
return once more to the role.

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:47.220
The addition of his theme music is just a
cherry on top of the brooding, crime-fighting sundae.

00:01:47.477 --> 00:01:51.960
Back in late November, amid questions about
which (if any) of Zack Snyder's "Justice League"

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:56.320
cast members would make the cut of James Gunn
and Peter Safran's new DC Universe, rumors

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.960
circulated that "The Flash" film would be
heavily edited to remove all references to

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.530
the Snyderverse to avoid making promises they
didn't intend to keep.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.750
For a brief moment, it appeared as though
these cuts would mean the omission of Ben

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.630
Affleck's Batman, who was previously announced
to appear.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:13.320
Thankfully, it seems that he not only made
the final cut, but looks to be getting a pretty

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.340
substantial amount of screen time.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:19.830
As Bruce Wayne (likely early on in the film),
he warns Barry about the dangers of tampering

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:20.830
with time.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.760
Later on in the trailer, we get a good look
at his brand-new bat suit.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.811
It appears to be a combination of the Snyder
"Justice League" design and some comic book

00:02:27.811 --> 00:02:31.810
influences, most likely inspired by the "Batman:
Hush" era from 2002.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:36.390
The book was drawn by iconic "Batman" artist
Jim Lee and featured a gray-and-blue costume

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.720
very similar to what Affleck's Batman can
be seen wearing in the trailer.

00:02:40.378 --> 00:02:44.360
One of the most critical aspects of the "Flashpoint"
timeline is the altered path the character

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:45.840
of Superman takes.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.959
While Kal-El is the big hitter of the Justice
League in the main DC continuity, in the "Flashpoint"

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:51.800
storyline, he landed in Metropolis.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.280
He spent his entire life living in a science
lab, being studied by shadowing government

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:57.280
entities.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.420
It isn't until the end of the story when Flash,
Cyborg, and Thomas Wayne as Batman discover

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:05.810
his location and break him out of the lab
that he becomes a big part of the climax.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:09.880
Of course, with the DCEU all but dead and
Henry Cavill's departure as the franchise's

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.570
Superman, a different path needed to be taken.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:16.090
This is where Sasha Calle comes in, taking
on the role of Supergirl.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:19.720
The trailer gives us our first real look at
the character, seeing her looking emaciated

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.780
first and then introducing herself after our
two Barrys break her out of the lab.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:27.610
Last but not least, we see her flying next
to the Barrys in an aircraft, showing off

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:28.620
her new super suit.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.690
While we know there are some significant differences
from the comic version to the film version

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:36.879
— including multiple Batmen, multiple Barry
Allens, and Supergirl, there still seems to

00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:39.041
be enough evidence of a faithful adaptation.

00:03:39.041 --> 00:03:41.393
"What did you do?!"

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.220
"The Flash" Super Bowl trailer reveals actions
have consequences.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:49.860
And one of the deadliest SnyderVerse baddies,
General Zod, returns because of Barry Allen's

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.080
time-traveling antics.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.930
At just over a minute into the trailer, Barry
witnesses a transmission similar to the one

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.480
Zod broadcast while searching for Kal-El in
"Man of Steel."

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.810
The footage suggests that the Flash has returned
to that same point in time, and, as a result,

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:08.428
resurrected one of Superman's greatest adversaries
or is simply encountering an alternate reality version.

00:04:08.428 --> 00:04:13.340
After that, the Kryptonian World Engine wreaks
havoc, as it did in "Man of Steel" and "Batman

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:15.040
v Superman: Dawn of Justice."

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.790
Barry exclaims, quote, "I completely broke
the universe."

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:23.070
The very next shot reveals Zod aboard a Kryptonian
Attack Ship as plumes of smoke and fire rise

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.500
from the ashes of a war zone below.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.100
Zod says as another Kryptonian vessel approaches,
"We've been waiting for you."

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.780
…but who is he speaking to?

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:34.910
It's unclear, but Zod's attack force lays
waste to the human soldiers desperately trying

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:35.758
to fight back.

00:04:35.758 --> 00:04:39.980
After two minutes in the trailer, a Kryptonian
ship hovers above what appears to be the Flash

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:44.400
clashing with either another Barry Allen or,
perhaps, the Reverse Flash.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.810
And then right after that, fans are treated
to arguably the best shot of the trailer:

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.610
Supergirl punches an armored-clad Zod right
in the face!

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.509
So, did the Flash's arrival in the past lead
to Kal-El's death?

00:04:55.509 --> 00:04:59.512
Whatever Barry did, it's now up to the Girl
of Steel to help stop Zod's tyranny.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.061
If you know anything about the Flash, then
you know that he has a sworn nemesis, one

00:05:04.061 --> 00:05:08.160
who would do anything to kill him and take
the title of "Fastest Man Alive" for his own.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.110
That's right, Eobard Thawne, also known as
the Reverse-Flash, might be the man behind

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:14.580
the curtain, though right now that's just
a theory.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.360
Known for going back in time and killing Barry's
mother, Thawne is vital to the "Flashpoint"

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:22.850
story and has appeared in every adaptation
of the story so far, including both live-action

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:24.460
and animation alike.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:28.160
With all that in mind, it would be surprising
if this yellow-and-red-clad speedster didn't

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.960
make an appearance in the upcoming movie,
and we may have already seen glimpses of him.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:35.949
Besides the brief flashes to the death of
Nora Allen that we see in the trailer.

00:05:35.949 --> 00:05:39.840
There's a strong possibility that the alternate
Barry could be the Reverse-Flash.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.237
In the comics, Thawne wants everything that
Barry has, and once even took over the speedster's life.

00:05:44.237 --> 00:05:48.100
In the original "Flashpoint" comic, Barry
is re-inserted into the new timeline after

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:52.869
he saves his mother, meaning that the inclusion
of the alternate Barry doesn't exactly make sense.

00:05:52.869 --> 00:05:56.710
Given the alternate Barry's yellow-themed
clothing, this may be a nod to his true identity

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:00.750
as Thawne, luring Barry into a sense of false
security before the final blow.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:05.640
He's been known to steal people's faces before,
and his jealousy towards the Flash knows no bounds.

00:06:06.258 --> 00:06:10.310
Of all the surprises in "The Flash" Super
Bowl TV Spot, none were more welcome than

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:12.148
all the Batman teases it contained.

00:06:12.148 --> 00:06:16.310
Probably the most exciting is that we got
a glimpse at Michael Keaton's different Batsuits,

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.410
many designed specifically for the film.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:22.150
In another time, these might have all been
made specifically to sell a boatload of toys,

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:25.740
but now they're just fabulous nods to bygone
eras of the Dark Knight.

00:06:26.383 --> 00:06:27.551
"Nice suit."

00:06:27.551 --> 00:06:28.491
"Thanks."

00:06:28.491 --> 00:06:32.680
While there are plenty of direct references
to previous Batman comics and uniforms, such

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:37.294
as the Neal Adams' era Batman with a blue
cowl, there's one that stands out above the rest.

00:06:37.294 --> 00:06:42.008
If you look closely at the Batsuit on the
far left, its logo doesn't quite match the others.

00:06:42.008 --> 00:06:46.248
That's because this is actually the bat-symbol
from the original Adam West "Batman" series.

00:06:46.248 --> 00:06:50.890
Costume designer Chris Weston confirmed this
on Twitter, revealing that the nod to the

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:52.790
'66 "Batman" was completely his idea.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:53.790
Weston explained,

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:55.180
"I was in fanboy heaven!

00:06:55.180 --> 00:07:00.680
Luckily I had a great lead designer, Alexandra
Byrne, who was very receptive to my wacky notions!"

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:04.789
Along with the nod to the original television
Batman, the suit's holsters, which hold grappling

00:07:04.789 --> 00:07:09.699
guns rather than twin pistols, and the classic
Golden Age "ears" were Weston's idea as well.

00:07:09.699 --> 00:07:13.310
The longer you look at these highly-detailed
uniforms, the more you see the comic book

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:14.310
influence on them.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:19.539
All this focus on Batsuits is fun and all,
but only true fans of the Flash comic books

00:07:19.539 --> 00:07:23.229
will note the insignia ring that Barry is
seen wearing throughout the trailer.

00:07:23.229 --> 00:07:26.910
In the comics, Barry created a super-suit
that could shrink itself into the size of

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:30.220
a ring that he would wear, so that he was
always prepared to save the day.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:34.470
This suit was a sort of friction-resistant
material that kept Barry protected when running

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:39.199
at top speeds, and even survived Barry's death
during the original "Crisis on Infinite Earths"

00:07:39.199 --> 00:07:40.699
comic book miniseries.

00:07:40.699 --> 00:07:44.800
On "The Flash" television show, Grant Gustin's
Barry Allen eventually gains a Flash ring

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:49.030
of his own after years of working on developing
one, and in the upcoming feature film, Ezra

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:52.440
Miller's Barry Allen seems to finally be doing
the same.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:56.100
Though he previously wore armor in "Justice
League" — inspired by his look during "The

00:07:56.100 --> 00:08:00.300
New 52" era — Barry has now built himself
a new uniform for "The Flash," one that's

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:04.409
sleeker, and better matches his traditional
comic book appearance, and, most importantly,

00:08:04.409 --> 00:08:05.669
fits within his Flash ring.

00:08:05.669 --> 00:08:09.729
Though we only get a few brief glimpses of
this Flash ring, it sports the hero's original

00:08:09.729 --> 00:08:12.949
insignia and opens at the lightning bolt to
reveal his new costume underneath.

00:08:12.949 --> 00:08:16.550
While we'll have to wait to see what the full
process looks like, it's exciting to think

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:19.620
about this small element of the Flash mythos
being translated to the big screen.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.482
It's about time.

00:08:22.159 --> 00:08:26.350
At this point, everybody knows that the catalyst
for Bruce Wayne's tenure as Batman is the

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:28.789
death of his parents, Thomas and Martha Wayne.

00:08:28.789 --> 00:08:33.019
It seems as if the death of one's parents
or mentor is something of a requirement for

00:08:33.019 --> 00:08:37.050
DC Comics superheroes, and it looks like "The
Flash" will revolve around this idea also.

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:40.550
While Barry is dealing with trying to save
his mother from her grisly fate, there's a

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:42.611
Batman out there still mourning his parents
too.

00:08:42.611 --> 00:08:48.420
"So why do you want to stay and fight to save
this one?"

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:50.940
"Because this is the one where my mom lives."

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:55.080
In a quick moment, we're shown a photograph
in Wayne Manor of a young boy with his parents.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:58.269
Given that this photo sits in front of the
room that contains all the Batsuits, it's

00:08:58.269 --> 00:09:00.520
more than likely that this is the Wayne family.

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:03.960
As we piece together this photo from the trailer
with the shot of Bruce entering his secret

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:08.420
chambers from the TV spot, this photo may
be the key to the Batcave itself.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:11.839
Fitting, given that Batman was born the night
his parents died.

00:09:11.839 --> 00:09:15.680
In the original "Flashpoint" story, it's revealed
that the Batman of the "Flashpoint" timeline

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:16.760
is actually Thomas Wayne.

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:19.589
It was his son Bruce that was killed that
night.

00:09:19.589 --> 00:09:23.010
It's not clear if that's the direction that
"The Flash" is taking with this timeline's

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:26.089
Batman, but Thomas still has an impact on
this fractured world.

00:09:26.089 --> 00:09:29.750
Here's hoping Keaton's Batman lives up to
Thomas' comic book mantle.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:35.529
In the Flashpoint-inspired world of "The Flash,"
Superman no longer exists.

00:09:35.529 --> 00:09:39.480
After the strange exit of Henry Cavill from
the DCEU, it should come as no surprise that

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:42.240
Superman isn't slated to appear in "The Flash."

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:46.850
Taking the Man of Steel's place in this reality-warping
story is his cousin, Kara Zor-El.

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:50.550
Known in the comics as Supergirl, Kara has
been a part of the Superman mythos for almost

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:54.830
as long as the Big Blue Boy Scout himself,
showing up in various live-action projects

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:56.080
before this one.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:00.019
Played by Sasha Calle in the upcoming film,
Kara's appearance is more akin to her famous

00:10:00.019 --> 00:10:03.420
cousin's look rather than her iconic comic
book counterpart.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:06.920
Even Melissa Benoist's Girl of Steel from
the "Supergirl" television series looked more

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:09.530
like the Kara Zor-El we know from the comics.

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:12.500
But just because this Woman of Tomorrow looks
a bit different doesn't mean that there isn't

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:14.420
comic book precedent for it.

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:17.370
After all, not every Supergirl sports the
long blonde look.

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:22.180
Both the Cir-El and Lara Lane-Kent versions
of Supergirl look remarkably similar to Calle's

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:23.310
version of the character.

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:27.730
Cir-El first appeared in "Superman: The 10-Cent
Adventure" #1 and eventually revealed herself

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:31.589
to be Superman's lost future daughter before
being erased from the timeline.

00:10:31.589 --> 00:10:36.010
Lara Lane-Kent made her debut in the "Injustice"
comic series as an imaginary daughter that

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:37.870
Superman might have had.

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:42.910
"Justice League" #34 later reveals that, in
some world, Lara is real, making her the version

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:44.460
that Calle takes the most cues from.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:50.120
Making their debut in the original 1989 "Batman,"
Michael Keaton's old vehicles are also set

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.610
to return in "The Flash," and they look just
as cool as ever.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:57.339
While recent films, beginning with Christopher
Nolan's "The Dark Knight" trilogy, have attempted

00:10:57.339 --> 00:11:01.800
to make Batman's primary modes of transportation
a bit more realistic, Tim Burton's original

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:05.029
Batmobile and Batwing were wonderfully impractical.

00:11:05.029 --> 00:11:09.149
But compared to some of the other Batmobiles
of late, the '89 Batmobile had style and even

00:11:09.149 --> 00:11:11.450
inspired the one from "Batman: The Animated
Series."

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:15.990
While Batman's "Flying Fox" from "Justice
League" is briefly seen, allowing Ben Affleck's

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:21.110
Batman to catch his prey, it's Michael Keaton's
Batwing that we're most excited to see take flight.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:25.240
The bat-shaped plane formerly flew up against
Jack Nicholson's Joker in "Batman," but hasn't

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:26.490
been seen much since.

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:30.441
Here, it looks as if the Batwing will serve
as some sort of electrical conduit, probably

00:11:30.441 --> 00:11:34.301
to give the alternate Barry Allen the same
Flash superpowers as the original, or, if

00:11:34.301 --> 00:11:39.120
we're following the "Flashpoint" comics here,
maybe it'll help restore Barry's lost abilities.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:42.870
Longtime Bat-fans are no doubt already excited
about "The Flash," but the return of the most

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:47.770
iconic-looking members of Batman's vehicular
arsenal is even more reason for excitement.

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:52.610
After all, the words "I'm Batman" don't mean
too much if there's nothing especially interesting

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:53.310
behind them.

